# Usage notes Old Wang explains (languages/zh/notes.json). note-<id>-title and note-<id>.

note-hao-ma-title = 好 and 吗
note-hao-ma = 好 (hǎo) means "good", and on its own it's how you agree: "OK". Put 吗 (ma) at the end of a sentence and it becomes a yes-or-no question: 你好吗？ is "How are you?", literally "You good?" The usual answer is 很好 (hěn hǎo), "very good"; the 很 is there mostly for rhythm.

note-lao-xiao-title = 老王 and 小张
note-lao-xiao = Friends and neighbours your own age are often called by surname with 老 (lǎo, "old") or 小 (xiǎo, "little") in front: 老王 for someone a bit older, 小张 for someone younger. From a young stranger to an old man it would be too familiar; you'd say 王大爷, "Grandpa Wang". Old Wang told you to call him 老王, which is his way of saying you're friends now. Calling you 小马 says the same.

note-le-title = 了 for a change
note-le = 了 (le) after a word often marks a change: 我饿了 is "I've got hungry", "I'm hungry now". You'll also hear it in 太好了！, "great!", literally "too good!"

note-bukeqi-title = 谢谢 and 不客气
note-bukeqi = 谢谢 (xièxie) is "thank you". The usual answer is 不客气 (bùkèqi), "you're welcome"; it literally means "don't be polite": no need to stand on ceremony. Between close friends, people often skip 谢谢 altogether; thanking a friend too much can sound distant.

note-bei-title = 杯, a measure word
note-bei = Chinese doesn't say "three teas". A measure word goes between a number and a thing: 三杯茶 is "three cups of tea". 杯 (bēi) is the one for cups and glasses. There are many measure words; 个 (gè) is the everyday all-purpose one.
